description:
## Ruling

**Retire reagent-slim before the first tag (rf2-kmqx3) publishes it to Clojars.** reagent-slim is `day8/reagent-slim`: the `reagent2.*` namespaces plus the `re-frame.adapter.reagent-slim` adapter, in `implementation/adapters/reagent-slim/`. After retirement:
- stock Reagent (`day8/re-frame2-reagent`) is the one Reagent adapter, and UIx and Fresco are unchanged;
- `:rf.adapter/reagent-slim` becomes a reserved, never-recycled kind, like `:rf.adapter/helix`;
- slim's leak-free renderer and its pure-CLJS HTML exporter go too. Git keeps both, so a later "Day8 owns its Reagent" decision can revive them.

Core seams: remove only `:adapter/as-element`, which exists solely because there were two Reagent runtimes. Its removal simplifies Xray's door. Leave everything else alone, because these pieces keep core free of any `reagent.*` require:
- the parameterised ratom spine;
- `:adapter/current-component`, `:adapter/ratom` and `:adapter/ratom?`.

This retirement deletes the code path behind `expand-tree`'s Form-3 arm, which today recognises only slim classes. Fix that arm here.

Decided 2026-10-16: Mike directed that every dossier recommendation an independent Codex review agreed with be actioned:

> review all decisions. They now have a "Codex Recomendation" section.  Where the original recomendation matches the Code Recomendation, create a bead to action the decision.  And then remove the file.  Leave behind cases where the two differ materially.

The dossier and the Codex review both chose option (A): retire before the first tag, reserve the adapter kind, move the docs playground to stock Reagent, and run no further study.

**Decisive facts, at trunk 89517e8f54:**
1. **The headline benefit measured at about 1 KB.** The same counter app under `:advanced` measured 184,268 B gzip on stock and 183,180 B on slim: 1,088 B, or −0.6% (rf2-kjx1 close, PR #9253, 2026-09-04). The 7-10 KB claim is labelled "analytical estimates, not Closure-built measurements", and its own threshold reads "If realised reduction is <15%, the slim framing is unsupported" (`implementation/adapters/reagent-slim/DESIGN-RATIONALE.md:341-348`).
2. **Its named audience cannot adopt it unchanged.** Slim is scoped to "re-com, re-frame-10x, Dash8, rf8" (`DESIGN-RATIONALE.md:20-22`), but its renderer captures derefs only against `reagent2.ratom/*ratom-context*` (`implementation/adapters/reagent-slim/src/reagent2/ratom.cljs:61`). That has two consequences for re-com:
   - its `reagent.core` requires pull stock Reagent back into the build;
   - its stock `reagent/atom` Form-2 local state would stop re-rendering.

   No app in the repo runs re-com on slim.
3. **Publishing is irreversible and collides on a namespace.** Slim is a `deploy-leaf` matrix entry (`.github/workflows/release.yml:420-423`). A release step then renames its adapter ns to `re-frame.adapter.reagent` (`release.yml:554-557`), which is the stock artefact's own namespace. Clojars has no yank.

**Rejected options:**
- **(B) Keep it first-class and correct the docs.** That publishes a coordinate whose stated benefit does not survive measurement, to an audience that cannot adopt it without a re-com port. It also keeps costing about one commit a day.
- **(C) Park it in the tree, unpublished and experimental.** That is a half-state with no end date: either its lanes keep costing, or the code rots.
- **(D) Run Sol's stock/slim/Fresco comparison first.** Its cheap half already exists (rf2-kjx1). Its expensive half needs a slim port of an app that does not exist.

**Supersedes** these rulings, as they apply to reagent-slim. Reagent and UIx keep their first-class standing.
- EP-0030 §Resolved Decisions "Adapter disposition reframed (2026-07-17)" (`docs/EP/EP-0030-the-compiled-view-substrate-program.md:339-351`), together with the "slim-bundle option" rationale at `:293-299`;
- HD-029(b) (`docs/design/fresco/decisions.md:2928`);
- the spec 006 adapter-disposition note (`spec/006-ReactiveSubstrate.md:1922`) and its inventory row (`:1931`);
- the CLAUDE.md Architecture Overview line that names reagent-slim first-class.

**What would reopen it:**
- a named application that needs leak-free Reagent-style local state or browser-side HTML export, with a runnable acceptance case;
- Mike deciding that Day8 owns its Reagent and ports re-com onto `reagent2`.

## Work

This bead is the ruling record, and it lands as ONE PR. Splitting it would leave the guard failing the next PR. The dossier proposed that the mayor land it. It is the first of three chained beads: this one, then the code/build/CI retirement, then the spec and docs sweep.

- **`docs/EP/EP-0030-the-compiled-view-substrate-program.md`.** Add a dated 2026-10-16 entry to §Resolved Decisions that:
  - retires reagent-slim;
  - names the reagent-slim clause of "Adapter disposition reframed (2026-07-17)" (`:339-351`) as superseded;
  - restates that stock Reagent and UIx stay first-class.

  The 2026-07-17 entry stays as the historical record.
- **`docs/design/fresco/decisions.md:2928` (HD-029(b)).** Add a short note saying that its reagent-slim clause is superseded by the 2026-10-16 retirement, and that Reagent and UIx are unaffected.
- **`CLAUDE.md` Architecture Overview.** The line "The `adapters/` (Reagent, reagent-slim, UIx) are first-class and actively supported" drops reagent-slim. Add nothing about its history; the Conventions tombstone carries that.
- **`scripts/check_adapter_disposition.py`.** Stop it protecting slim, and keep it enforcing Reagent and UIx:
  - the refusal "reagent-slim is NOT deleted" (`:128-136`);
  - the "only Helix is removed; reagent-slim is retained" pattern (`:138-145`);
  - the `EP0030_REQUIRED` assertion "EP-0030 must name reagent-slim among the retained adapters" (`:218-219`);
  - every message that lists "Reagent, reagent-slim, and UIx" (`:95`, `:122`, `:127`, `:165`);
  - the module docstring (`:5-13`);
  - the self-tests that pin slim (`:345`, `:396`).

  The guard's closed roster (`:76-85`) is:
  - EP-0030;
  - `skills/re-frame2-implementor/references/phase-2-impl-order.md`;
  - `implementation/README.md`;
  - `implementation/adapters/reagent/README.md`;
  - `.github/workflows/release.yml`.

  Later beads will write "reagent-slim is retired" into those files, and after this bead that text must pass.

**Deliberately stays:**
- the guard itself, its closed roster, and its EP-0030 positive assertion for Reagent and UIx;
- every other EP-0030 entry.

## Sequencing

- **Hot zones:** none of the CLAUDE.md hot-zone files.
- **Bead dependencies:** none. The code/build/CI retirement bead depends on this one.
- **Other dossiers:** run sequentially with fresco-taught-default-or-optional.md's ruling bead, never in parallel. Both edit `scripts/check_adapter_disposition.py` (its "only taught layer" and "replaces the adapter trio" patterns name slim), EP-0030 and HD-029. This retirement gates the first tag, so it goes first.
- **First tag:** the chain ends in a bead that blocks rf2-kmqx3.

## Evidence

**Read in source at trunk 89517e8f54:**
- the guard's slim clauses and self-tests;
- EP-0030 `:293-299` and `:339-351`;
- HD-029(b) at `decisions.md:2928`;
- the CLAUDE.md line;
- `release.yml:420-423` and `:554-557`;
- the rf2-kjx1 close-reason figures.

**Needs a run:** the edited guard's self-tests and a tree run.

Entering a compound through an :initial that names its own history pseudo-state commits the pseudo-state as the active leaf. Spec 005 says a history pseudo-state is never occupied (Restoring, "In every case the resolved leaf path"), and its Restoring section now says such an entry resolves like a transition naming the pseudo-state: the recorded configuration, else :default-target. W3C SCXML test580 tests exactly this shape (a parallel region's compound whose initial targets its history, asserting the history is never in the configuration).

Measured on the JVM at trunk 6b26ad25ca with a compound {:initial :sh :states {:sh {:type :history :default-target :b} :a {} :b {} :c {...}}} under a root {:initial :park :states {:park {:on {:enter-group :group :enter-hist [:group :sh]}} :group ...}}:
- registration (validate-machine!) accepts it;
- transition [:enter-group] from :park, no recording: :state [:group :sh] (should be [:group :b]);
- the same with :rf/history {[:group] :c}: :state [:group :sh] (should be [:group :c :c1]);
- the explicit target [:enter-hist] resolves correctly: [:group :b] with no recording, [:group :c :c1] with one;
- birth with root :initial :group: [:group :b] with :default-target, but [:group :sh] when the pseudo-state has no :default-target.

