More evidence from the same downstream migration's skills retrospective (suggestion, not a work order). Verified at origin/main d53a3faf09: guided-handlers-state.md:214 and orchestrating-a-large-migration.md §5 are unchanged since 0783705626.

Two units add to this item, one from each end.

1. **A route that runs inside a unit.** One unit produced M-42 evidence mid-sweep without a compiling app. It used a scratch build: a node target whose only entry was the producer's namespace plus a small render script, and nothing else of the app. That build rendered the producer to static markup under the old Reagent/React pair and under the new one, and diffed the two. The output was byte-identical. This works whenever the producer is a pure function of its props and does not reach the half-converted rest of the tree.
   Suggested addition to guided-handlers-state.md:214: "In a fan-out where the app cannot compile yet, render the producer in an isolated scratch build under the v1 and the v2 Reagent/React pairs (for example to static markup) and compare. Run the remove-the-tag red/green in the same build. Where the producer's namespace cannot compile on its own, record the evidence plan instead (see the suggested change above)."

2. **The deferred rows need one fixed place and one schema.** On the same migration's convergence pass, collecting every unit's deferred runtime checks took a dedicated pass over 17 unit PRs, because the units used 5 different headings for them. Suggested addition to the bullet above: prescribe one section in every unit's deliverable (for example "## Runtime checks for the convergence smoke"), with one row per check:
   • surface or page;
   • control or trigger;
   • rule id;
   • expected result;
   • whether v1 showed the same result (baseline y/n).
   The convergence gate then reads one known section per unit. This generalises the M-42 evidence plan to every rule whose check needs a running tree: M-42 producers, M-11 callback sites, and the async entry points of runtime-smoke-test.md step 6.

Observed on a ~170-file app.
